## Why This Is Asked
Tests a common guard-rail pattern (flags) with practical, beginner-friendly steps.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags in E2E context
- Test data isolation and seeding
- API interception/mocking
- Verifying multiple UX paths in one file

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright sketch
test('onboarding flag true', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/api/flags/**', async route => {
    route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ TutorialEnabled: true }) });
  });
  // simulate signup
  await page.locator('#signup-email').fill('user@example.com');
  await page.locator('#signup-password').fill('Passw0rd!');
  await page.locator('#signup-button').click();
  await expect(page.locator('.tutorial-carousel')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend for flaky network conditions?
- How would you run both variants in parallel across browsers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests spanning multi-tenant boundaries and asynchronous export flows surface data isolation and resilience issues. This question probes end-to-end coverage for real-world SaaS data export use cases like Airbnb and Microsoft enterprise products.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data isolation across services
- Async background jobs and eventual consistency
- End-to-end validation of blob storage and email delivery
- Idempotent retries and fault injection
- Test data strategy and environment isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test outline (Playwright + API stubs)
test('tenant-scoped data export end-to-end', async ({ page, api, storage }) => {
  await loginAsAdmin(page);
  await page.click('#start-export');
  await page.waitForResponse(r => r.url.includes('/exports/').and(r.status() === 200));
  const status = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
  const blob = await storage.getBlob(`exports/${tenantId}/data.json`);
  expect(blob).toContain(`"tenantId":"${tenantId}"`);
  // idempotence
  await page.click('#start-export');
  const status2 = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status2).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you parameterize test data for tenants with different data shapes?
- How would you enforce data privacy in the test environment while validating exports?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first PWAs are a common beginner challenge, ensuring service workers and syncing work under real user conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service worker caching
- E2E data seeding for deterministic tests
- Network control via intercepts (cy.intercept / route)
- Idempotent sync and duplicate prevention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress example sketch
cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', {user:'test', products:[101,102]});
cy.visit('/');
cy.intercept('/api/cart', { statusCode: 503 }).as('cartOffline');
cy.get('#add-101').click();
cy.get('#add-102').click();
cy.intercept('/api/cart', (req) => req.continue()).as('cartOnline');
cy.wait('@cartOffline');
cy.get('#checkout').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a multi-user scenario with concurrent offline sessions?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the offline sync is fast enough for a good UX?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-time streaming paths, backpressure management, reconnection handling, and cross-service visibility in a realistic production-like setup.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time data ingestion over WebSocket
- Normalization to canonical schema
- Downstream fan-out via message bus with backpressure
- Burst load simulation, tick miss detection, and reconnection handling
- End-to-end latency SLA verification and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Synthetic tick generator
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://tdp.example');
ws.onopen = () => {
  for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ symbol: 'AAPL', price: 150 + Math.random(), ts: Date.now() }));
  }
};

// Verify end-to-end latency
const tickSent = Date.now();
ws.onmessage = (msg) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - JSON.parse(msg.data).sentAt;
  if (latency > SLA_THRESHOLD_MS) alert(`SLA breach: ${latency}ms`);
};

// Reconnection with missed-tick detection
ws.onclose = () => reconnect(lastSequenceId);
```

